    "result_text": "There's a single test class, and it passes. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **New file `LocalityResolver.java`** (mapper package) \u2014 a small, cohesive helper. `resolve(city, postcode)` maps a 4-digit postcode by range (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099) first, and only falls back to the fixed city-to-region table when the postcode is absent or in no known range; yields `UNKNOWN` when neither identifies a region.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `deriveLocality` now delegates to `LocalityResolver.resolve(...)` (one-line body change). Removed the now-unused `CITY_REGION` field and its `java.util.Map` import.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:** all new behaviour (the two tables plus the postcode/city branching) lives in the new class, which has near-zero surrounding complexity. The only edit inside the large `OwnerMapper` interface is a single-line, CC-1 method body, keeping `WMC_other * CC * changed_lines` minimal there.\n\nKnown cities still return the same region; a VIC-range postcode on an otherwise-unknown city now correctly yields `VIC`. The existing postcode *validation* advice was left untouched \u2014 it's a separate concern (rejecting out-of-range postcodes for a known city), and touching it would only add cost without being required.\n\nTest suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 2/2 tests pass (including the new `coreRegionFromPostcodeRange` and `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity`).",
